{"authored" [] "sources" [{"question" "q-6669" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "For the polynomial $f(x)={x}^{3}-10{x}^{2}+27x-18,$ how many *potential* roots are given by the rational roots theorem?" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/13968741/state.json" "file_sha256" "00199b84327238a14cca117774a6ee339293a80045f258a5fa09f95081a91a6c"} {"question" "q-6669" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "Given a polynomial with integer coefficients, the rational roots theorem states that any rational roots of the polynomial must take the form\n\n$$\n\\pm \\frac{\\text{factor of the constant term}}{\\text{factor of the leading coefficient}}.\n$$\n\nFor the given polynomial:\n\n- The constant term is $-18,$ and its factors are $\\pm 1,\\,\\pm 2,\\,\\pm 3,\\,\\pm 6,\\,\\pm 9$ and  $\\pm 18.$\n\n- The leading coefficient is $1,$ and its factors are $\\pm 1.$\n\nFirst, we list out all the possibilities for the numerator and denominator:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\pm \\frac{1}{1}, & \\pm \\frac{2}{1}, & \\pm \\frac{3}{1}, & \\pm \\frac{6}{1}, & \\pm \\frac{9}{1}, & \\pm \\frac{18}{1}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNext, we reduce the fractions to lowest terms:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\pm 1, & \\pm 2, & \\pm 3, & \\pm 6, & \\pm 9, & \\pm 18.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNotice that none of these potential roots are duplicates. \n\nThis makes a total of $12$ potential roots. (Remember that each $\\pm $ sign accounts for $2$ roots, and $2\\cdot 6=12.$)" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/13968741/state.json" "file_sha256" "00199b84327238a14cca117774a6ee339293a80045f258a5fa09f95081a91a6c"} {"question" "q-6669" "field" nil "attribute" "question/difficulty" "value" :question.difficulty/moderate "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/13968741/activity-metadata.json" "file_sha256" "09da52ec8865d11acc0960e8d9ea400e484a3f1141a45b190a80c3766ded3f7f"} {"question" "q-6669" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/type" "value" :answer-field.type/radio "category" "ma_widget" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/13968741/state.json" "file_sha256" "00199b84327238a14cca117774a6ee339293a80045f258a5fa09f95081a91a6c"} {"question" "q-6669" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/choices" "value" [["math" "10"] ["math" "12"] ["math" "3"] ["math" "6"] ["math" "8"]] "category" "ma_complete_choices" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/13968741/state.json" "file_sha256" "00199b84327238a14cca117774a6ee339293a80045f258a5fa09f95081a91a6c"} {"question" "q-6669" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/correct" "value" [:answer.type/math "12"] "category" "ma_successful_grade" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/13968741/state.json" "file_sha256" "00199b84327238a14cca117774a6ee339293a80045f258a5fa09f95081a91a6c"} {"question" "q-83840" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "For the polynomial $f(x)=2{x}^{5}+21{x}^{3}+16x+14,$ how many *potential* roots are given by the rational roots theorem?" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/13968741/state.json" "file_sha256" "00199b84327238a14cca117774a6ee339293a80045f258a5fa09f95081a91a6c"} {"question" "q-83840" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "Given a polynomial with integer coefficients, the rational roots theorem states that any rational roots of the polynomial must take the form\n\n$$\n\\pm \\frac{\\text{factor of the constant term}}{\\text{factor of the leading coefficient}}.\n$$\n\nFor the given polynomial:\n\n- The constant term is $14,$ and its factors are $\\pm 1,$ $\\pm 2,$ $\\pm 7,$ and $\\pm 14.$\n\n- The leading coefficient is $2,$ and its factors are $\\pm 1,$ $\\pm 2.$\n\nFirst, we list out all the possibilities for the numerator and denominator:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\pm \\frac{1}{1}, & \\pm \\frac{1}{2}, \\\\ \\pm \\frac{2}{1}, & \\pm \\frac{2}{2}, \\\\ \\pm \\frac{7}{1}, & \\pm \\frac{7}{2}, \\\\ \\pm \\frac{14}{1}, & \\pm \\frac{14}{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNext, we reduce the fractions to lowest terms:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\pm 1, & \\pm \\frac{1}{2}, \\\\ \\pm 2, & \\pm 1, \\\\ \\pm 7, & \\pm \\frac{7}{2}, \\\\ \\pm 14, & \\pm 7.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNotice that several potential roots are duplicates (shown above in red). Removing these duplicates, and listing those remaining in order from least to greatest in absolute value, we have\n\n$$\n\\pm \\frac{1}{2},\\,\\pm 1,\\,\\pm 2,\\,\\pm \\frac{7}{2}\\,\\pm 7,\\,\\pm 14.\n$$\n\nThis makes a total of $12$ potential roots. (Remember that each $\\pm $ sign accounts for $2$ roots, and $2\\cdot 6=12.$)" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/13968741/state.json" "file_sha256" "00199b84327238a14cca117774a6ee339293a80045f258a5fa09f95081a91a6c"} {"question" "q-83840" "field" nil "attribute" "question/difficulty" "value" :question.difficulty/moderate "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/13968741/activity-metadata.json" "file_sha256" "09da52ec8865d11acc0960e8d9ea400e484a3f1141a45b190a80c3766ded3f7f"} {"question" "q-83840" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/type" "value" :answer-field.type/radio "category" "ma_widget" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/13968741/state.json" "file_sha256" "00199b84327238a14cca117774a6ee339293a80045f258a5fa09f95081a91a6c"} {"question" "q-83840" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/choices" "value" [["math" "10"] ["math" "12"] ["math" "14"] ["math" "6"] ["math" "8"]] "category" "ma_complete_choices" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/13968741/state.json" "file_sha256" "00199b84327238a14cca117774a6ee339293a80045f258a5fa09f95081a91a6c"} {"question" "q-83840" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/correct" "value" [:answer.type/math "12"] "category" "model_interpretation" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/13968741/state.json" "file_sha256" "00199b84327238a14cca117774a6ee339293a80045f258a5fa09f95081a91a6c"} {"question" "q-112874" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "According to the rational roots theorem, which of the following **cannot** be a root of the polynomial $f(x)=10{x}^{5}-7{x}^{2}+2x-8?$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/13968741/state.json" "file_sha256" "00199b84327238a14cca117774a6ee339293a80045f258a5fa09f95081a91a6c"} {"question" "q-112874" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "Given a polynomial with integer coefficients, the rational roots theorem states that any rational roots of the polynomial must take the form\n\n$$\n\\pm \\frac{\\text{factor of the constant term}}{\\text{factor of the leading coefficient}}.\n$$\n\nFor the given polynomial $f(x),$ the constant term is $-8$ and the leading coefficient is $10.$ So, any rational roots of $f(x)$ must take the form\n\n$$\n\\pm \\frac{\\text{factor of }-8}{\\text{factor of }10}.\n$$\n\nOf the given answer choices, only $-\\frac{6}{5}=\\frac{-6}{5}$ does not take the above form. The numerator $(-6)$ is not a factor of $-8.$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/13968741/state.json" "file_sha256" "00199b84327238a14cca117774a6ee339293a80045f258a5fa09f95081a91a6c"} {"question" "q-112874" "field" nil "attribute" "question/difficulty" "value" :question.difficulty/moderate "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/13968741/activity-metadata.json" "file_sha256" "09da52ec8865d11acc0960e8d9ea400e484a3f1141a45b190a80c3766ded3f7f"} {"question" "q-112874" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/type" "value" :answer-field.type/radio "category" "ma_widget" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/13968741/state.json" "file_sha256" "00199b84327238a14cca117774a6ee339293a80045f258a5fa09f95081a91a6c"} {"question" "q-112874" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/choices" "value" [["math" "-4"] ["math" "-\\frac{1}{10}"] ["math" "-\\frac{6}{5}"] ["math" "2"] ["math" "\\frac{2}{5}"]] "category" "ma_complete_choices" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/13968741/state.json" "file_sha256" "00199b84327238a14cca117774a6ee339293a80045f258a5fa09f95081a91a6c"} {"question" "q-112874" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/correct" "value" [:answer.type/math "-\\frac{6}{5}"] "category" "ma_successful_grade" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/13968741/state.json" "file_sha256" "00199b84327238a14cca117774a6ee339293a80045f258a5fa09f95081a91a6c"} {"question" "q-112875" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "According to the rational roots theorem, which of the following **cannot** be a root of the polynomial $f(x)=2{x}^{3}-3{x}^{2}+6x-4?$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/13968741/state.json" "file_sha256" "00199b84327238a14cca117774a6ee339293a80045f258a5fa09f95081a91a6c"} {"question" "q-112875" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "Given a polynomial with integer coefficients, the rational roots theorem states that any rational roots of the polynomial must take the form\n\n$$\n\\pm \\frac{\\text{factor of the constant term}}{\\text{factor of the leading coefficient}}.\n$$\n\nFor the given polynomial $f(x),$ the constant term is $-4$ and the leading coefficient is $2.$ So, any rational roots of $f(x)$ must take the form\n\n$$\n\\pm \\frac{\\text{factor of }-4}{\\text{factor of }2}.\n$$\n\nOf the given answer choices, only $-3$ does not take the above form. The value $-3$ can be interpreted as a fraction $-\\frac{3}{1},$ and the numerator $(3)$ is not a factor of $-4.$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/13968741/state.json" "file_sha256" "00199b84327238a14cca117774a6ee339293a80045f258a5fa09f95081a91a6c"} {"question" "q-112875" "field" nil "attribute" "question/difficulty" "value" :question.difficulty/moderate "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/13968741/activity-metadata.json" "file_sha256" "09da52ec8865d11acc0960e8d9ea400e484a3f1141a45b190a80c3766ded3f7f"} {"question" "q-112875" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/type" "value" :answer-field.type/radio "category" "ma_widget" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/13968741/state.json" "file_sha256" "00199b84327238a14cca117774a6ee339293a80045f258a5fa09f95081a91a6c"} {"question" "q-112875" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/choices" "value" [["math" "-1"] ["math" "-3"] ["math" "-\\frac{1}{2}"] ["math" "2"] ["math" "4"]] "category" "ma_complete_choices" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/13968741/state.json" "file_sha256" "00199b84327238a14cca117774a6ee339293a80045f258a5fa09f95081a91a6c"} {"question" "q-112875" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/correct" "value" [:answer.type/math "-3"] "category" "model_interpretation" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/13968741/state.json" "file_sha256" "00199b84327238a14cca117774a6ee339293a80045f258a5fa09f95081a91a6c"} {"question" "q-112984" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "Given that the polynomial $f(x)=4{x}^{3}-15{x}^{2}+12x+4$ has a root in the interval $(-\\frac{1}{2},0),$ find the sum of the **distinct** real roots of $f(x).$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/13968741/state.json" "file_sha256" "00199b84327238a14cca117774a6ee339293a80045f258a5fa09f95081a91a6c"} {"question" "q-112984" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "Given a polynomial with integer coefficients, the rational roots theorem states that any rational roots of the polynomial must take the form\n\n$$\n\\pm \\frac{\\text{factor of the constant term}}{\\text{factor of the leading coefficient}}.\n$$\n\nFor the given polynomial $f(x),$ the constant term is $4$ and the leading coefficient is $4.$ So, any rational roots of $f(x)$ must take the form\n\n$$\n\\pm \\frac{\\text{factor of }4}{\\text{factor of }4}.\n$$\n\nTherefore, the possible rational roots are as follows:\n\n$$\n\\pm \\frac{1}{4},\\,\\pm \\frac{1}{2},\\,\\pm 1,\\,\\pm 2,\\,\\pm 4.\n$$\n\nOf these roots, the only root in the interval $(-\\frac{1}{2},0)$ is $-\\frac{1}{4}.$\n\nUsing the synthetic division, we get the following:\n\n|  | ${x}^{3}$ | ${x}^{2}$ | ${x}^{1}$ | ${x}^{0}$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| $-\\frac{1}{4}$ | $4$ | $-15$ | $12$ | $4$ |\n|  | $↓$ | $-1$ | $4$ | $-4$ |\n|  | $4$ | $-16$ | $16$ | $0$ |\n\nTherefore, we have\n\n$$\nf(x)=(x+\\frac{1}{4})(4{x}^{2}-16x+16).\n$$\n\nSince the highest-degree factor is now a quadratic, we can continue factoring using the usual methods:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(x) & =(x+\\frac{1}{4})(4{x}^{2}-16x+16) \\\\  & =4(x+\\frac{1}{4})({x}^{2}-4x+4) \\\\  & =(4x+1)(x-2)^{2}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSetting the factors equal to $0,$ we find the roots $x=-\\frac{1}{4},2.$ Summing these roots, we get\n\n$$\n-\\frac{1}{4}+2=\\frac{7}{4}.\n$$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/13968741/state.json" "file_sha256" "00199b84327238a14cca117774a6ee339293a80045f258a5fa09f95081a91a6c"} {"question" "q-112984" "field" nil "attribute" "question/difficulty" "value" :question.difficulty/hard "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/13968741/activity-metadata.json" "file_sha256" "09da52ec8865d11acc0960e8d9ea400e484a3f1141a45b190a80c3766ded3f7f"} {"question" "q-112984" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/type" "value" :answer-field.type/radio "category" "ma_widget" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/13968741/state.json" "file_sha256" "00199b84327238a14cca117774a6ee339293a80045f258a5fa09f95081a91a6c"} {"question" "q-112984" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/choices" "value" [["math" "-2"] ["math" "-\\frac{5}{2}"] ["math" "0"] ["math" "1"] ["math" "\\frac{7}{4}"]] "category" "ma_complete_choices" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/13968741/state.json" "file_sha256" "00199b84327238a14cca117774a6ee339293a80045f258a5fa09f95081a91a6c"} {"question" "q-112984" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/correct" "value" [:answer.type/math "\\frac{7}{4}"] "category" "ma_successful_grade" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/13968741/state.json" "file_sha256" "00199b84327238a14cca117774a6ee339293a80045f258a5fa09f95081a91a6c"}] "usage" {} "basis" 656 "retractions" [] "immutable_answers" [] "immutable_fields" [] "no_history" []}
